    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Plasma Membrane",
    "stem": "A cell biologist studies a protein embedded in the plasma membrane. Its amino acid chain has three regions in order: an N-terminal region rich in polar and charged R groups, a middle stretch of about 20 amino acids with mostly hydrophobic R groups (just long enough to cross the bilayer once), and a C-terminal region rich in polar and charged R groups. Which description best explains how this protein is oriented in the membrane?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The hydrophilic regions sit in the bilayer interior, and the hydrophobic middle stretch is exposed on the membrane surface.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the bilayer. It treats the membrane interior as hydrophilic, perhaps because the phosphate heads are pictured as the membrane's core. In fact the hydrocarbon tails make the interior hydrophobic, so charged R groups would be unstable there. Fix: Sketch the bilayer with heads facing water on both sides before placing any protein region."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The whole protein rests on the outer surface, bound to the phospholipid heads, without entering the bilayer.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes every membrane protein is hydrophilic and sits only on the surface. That would leave the hydrophobic stretch exposed to water, which is unfavorable. Hydrophobic R groups are stabilized by contact with the fatty acid tails, so that stretch must lie inside the bilayer. Fix: Check each region's R groups and match hydrophobic regions to the tails."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The hydrophobic middle stretch lies in the bilayer interior, and both hydrophilic regions project into the extracellular fluid on the same side.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The interior placement is correct, but the student did not follow the chain through the membrane. The stretch crosses the bilayer only once, so the region before it and the region after it must end up on opposite faces. They cannot both be on one side. Fix: Count how many times the chain crosses the bilayer and trace which side each end reaches."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The hydrophobic middle stretch lies among the fatty acid tails in the bilayer interior, and the two hydrophilic regions extend into the aqueous fluid on opposite sides of the membrane.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Phospholipid tails form a hydrophobic core, while the heads face water on both sides. Hydrophobic R groups are stable only among the tails, and polar or charged R groups are stable in water. One crossing therefore places one hydrophilic end in the extracellular fluid and the other in the cytosol."
     }
    ]
   },
