   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Plasma Membrane",
    "stem": "Researchers observe a protein that spans a cell’s plasma membrane, with regions exposed to the watery environments on both sides. The protein changes its position within the membrane while remaining embedded. Which explanation best accounts for both its orientation and its movement?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The phospholipid bilayer has heads facing water and tails facing inward. The protein’s hydrophobic regions contact the tails, its hydrophilic regions face water, and it can move laterally within the fluid membrane.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The bilayer’s arrangement follows from phospholipids having both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions. Matching protein regions to these environments explains how the protein remains embedded, while the fluid mosaic model explains its lateral movement."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The phospholipid bilayer has tails facing water and heads facing inward. The protein’s hydrophilic regions contact the heads inside the membrane, and it can move laterally within the fluid membrane.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-bilayer trap: This places hydrophobic tails in water and hydrophilic heads away from water. Phospholipids instead orient their heads toward the watery environments on both sides. Fix: Sketch water on both sides of the membrane, then place each phospholipid region according to its interaction with water."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The phospholipid bilayer has heads facing water and tails facing inward. The protein’s hydrophilic regions preferentially contact the tails, its hydrophobic regions face water, and it can move laterally within the fluid membrane.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-protein-polarity trap: This correctly arranges the phospholipids but reverses the protein’s compatible interactions. Hydrophobic protein regions associate with the hydrophobic interior, while hydrophilic regions can face the watery environments. Fix: Match each protein region’s water affinity to the membrane environment it contacts."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The phospholipid bilayer has heads facing water and tails facing inward. The protein’s hydrophobic regions contact the tails and its hydrophilic regions face water, but the membrane framework permanently fixes every protein in place.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Static-membrane trap: Correct polarity matching does not make the membrane a rigid framework. Permanently fixing every protein in place contradicts both the observed movement and the fluid mosaic model. Fix: Check whether your membrane model allows the observed protein to change position within the bilayer."
     }
    ]
   },
